  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 105 PM CST Fri Nov 27 2026\n\nToday and tonight... A weak surface trough associated with a \nlow over southern Canada will continue to moves across the Upper\nMidwest the rest of today into tonight. This will provide some \nsupport for showers today into tonight. Saturation is the \nquestion though, as soundings show weak depth of saturation in \nthe atmosphere. Enough for cloud cover, as seen on current \nsatellite, but not enough for high confidence in rain. This is \nalso what has been seen with some isolated light rain being \nreported. These showers will track east the rest of today into \ntonight as the overall system tracks to the east. It will be \ntonight where there could be some impacts with this as it gets\ncolder. Surface temperatures are forecast to fall faster than \n850 mb temperatures and this warm nose could provide a setup for\nfreezing rain. The overall movement of the system would have \nthe showers in western Wisconsin by the time temperatures cool \nenough for this to happen. Thankfully overall moisture still \nisn't high so freezing rain accumulations would only be for a \nfew hundredths making a light glaze on untreated surfaces at\nmost. Overall confidence on the temperature profile is high, \nbut uncertainty remains in the moisture. If precipitation does \noccur tonight in western Wisconsin it will likely be freezing \nrain.\n\nThis weekend... Saturday looks to be a very nice day, especially\nfor late November standards with highs in the 40s. Exactly how\nhigh we get will depend on when the clouds move in. An earlier\narrival will suppress the warming and a later arrival will\nincrease the warming. Going back to the cloud cover, it will\nclear out behind today's system. Then more clouds will move in\non Saturday and continue into Sunday as colder air moves\nassociated with the cold front from the previously mentioned low\nover Canada. This cold air advection will bring temperatures\ndown with Sunday's highs in the low to mid 30s. We also should\nsee some gustier winds Sunday with gusts up around 20 mph\nassociated with this same cold air advection as it will promote\nmixing. Lastly moisture will still be a question for any\nprecipitation chances this weekend as this front moves through.\nSome low PoPs were maintained, but confidence remains low for a\nsimilar depth of moisture question like today. The better\nchances for Saturday night into Sunday morning looks to be to\nour south closer to a surface low that is forecast to move\nalong/near I-70.\n\nNext week... The cold air continued to advect in on Monday\nkeeping temperatures cooler and in the 30s. A change in flow\nwill return us to a warming trend Tuesday into Wednesday with\nabove normal highs returning. Temperatures pushing 50 degrees\nare expected across southern Minnesota. The overall pattern \nappears to be zonal flow with little to no signs of any waves. \nIt won't be until a larger system following a Colorado Low \narchetype late next week that we see chances for precipitation \nreturn. This system also is not a guarantee for us, as it could \neasily remain to our south. This overall setup keeps the record \nchance alive for the latest measurable snow for the Twin Cities.\nThe current record is December 3 from 1928 and our current \nforecast would support breaking that record. Looking at \nensembles, only isolated outlier members in some members of the\nEPS give MSP Airport snow by this time. Not a lock yet, but it\ncertainly is looking more and more likely each day that this\nlatest measurable snow record will be set.",
